<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!-- Wielder-side vehicle for UMW_Quilloned's dodge buff. (The hediff keeps its "parrying" name:
       it names the wielder's act, the way ability defs do — the trait names the hardware.)
       The hediff is the IDIOMATIC vehicle here, not a workaround (full 1.6 decompile, 2026-07-27):
       WeaponTraitDef.equippedStatOffsets is consumed in exactly two places in the assembly, both
       hardcoded to TryGetComp<CompBladelinkWeapon> (StatWorker.StatOffsetFromGear /
       GearHasCompsThatAffectStat) — silently inert on CompUniqueWeapon. The gear stat pipe is also
       offsets-only (gear can never contribute a factor) and its stat-breakdown line aggregates every
       offset on the item into one sum under the weapon's generated unique name, with no per-trait
       attribution. equippedHediffs, by contrast, are applied/removed by VANILLA's own
       WeaponTraitWorker.Notify_Equipped/Notify_EquipmentLost via CompUniqueWeapon — the only
       trait-granular wielder vehicle it has, the only one supporting statFactors, and its breakdown
       line is named per-hediff ("parrying guard: +3"). Don't migrate this to equippedStatOffsets.
       The +3 is in MeleeDodgeChance's UNFINALIZED units, not percent. Hediff statOffsets are summed
       by StatWorker.GetValueUnfinalized and only then does FinalizeValue apply the stat's
       postProcessCurve ((5,0) (20,0.30) (60,0.50)), so one raw point is worth ~2 percentage points of
       dodge across melee 5-20 and ~0.5pp above it, and nothing at all below raw 5 where the curve
       floors. Raw equals the wielder's melee skill level for an intact pawn: PawnCapacityOffset.GetOffset
       is (efficiency - 1) * scale, so healthy Moving/Sight contribute zero. +3 therefore reads as
       +6pp for an ordinary wielder decaying to ~+1.5pp for a melee specialist — the guard helps the
       conscript more than the master, which suits the fiction.
       Sized against the only two sources of this stat in Core+DLC: Nimble's +15 and Ideology
       BerserkTrance's +8 — 1/5 of the character-defining trait, under half the temporary trance.
       No vanilla weapon or apparel grants dodge at all, so there is no weapon-borne precedent to
       match. An earlier +2 held a ~1/8-of-Nimble ceiling; the 2026-07 Odyssey cross-check showed
       Odyssey prices a weapon trait at 1/2-1x a comparable character trait (AimAssistance's x1.2
       all-range accuracy is a whole shooting trait's worth), so 1/8 was over-conservative — and a
       melee wielder is guaranteed to trade blows, so survivability is the melee analog of ranged
       tempo (QuickReload, whose MV +50 the trait matches). An earlier 0.05 before that was written
       as though the field took a percentage and bought +0.1pp, i.e. nothing.
       The info card renders this with finalized:false (TraitEffectSummary.StatLine), so it shows as
       "+3.0" — the same way vanilla shows Nimble's +15, not as a percentage. -->
  <HediffDef>
    <defName>UMW_ParryingGuard</defName>
    <label>parrying guard</label>
    <description>The hand-guard lets the wielder catch incoming blows and turn them aside.</description>
    <hediffClass>Hediff</hediffClass>
    <everCurableByItem>false</everCurableByItem>
    <isBad>false</isBad>
    <stages>
      <li>
        <statOffsets>
          <MeleeDodgeChance>3</MeleeDodgeChance> <!-- raw, pre-curve: ~+6pp dodge. cf. Nimble +15 -->
        </statOffsets>
      </li>
    </stages>
  </HediffDef>

</Defs>
